{:edb/db-before-t 151 :edb/db-after-t 152 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533464 50 #inst "2026-10-10T23:33:06.390Z" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186054830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392030 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186054830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392037 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186059748 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392965 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186059760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393298 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186059778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393228 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186059778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393235 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186059778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393242 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186059782 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393305 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186059782 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393312 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186059782 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393319 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186064167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392655 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186064215 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392695 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186064375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393249 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186064381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392648 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186064952 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063738 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186064952 1068 #edb/ref 17592186393180 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186064952 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186064952 1071 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in centimeters) at time $t$ seconds is given by\n$s(t) = \\frac{5t - 10}{t}, t > 0$.\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t = 2s$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186064952 1071 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in centimeters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=\\frac{5t-10}{t},\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186064952 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5t-10}{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=2,$ the velocity is\n\n$$\nv(2)=\\frac{10}{(2)^{2}}=\\frac{10}{4}=2.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|2.5|=2.5\\,\\text{cm/s}.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186068693 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392574 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186068701 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392621 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186068702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392608 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186068863 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393479 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186068926 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392721 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186069375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067390 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186069375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392615 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186069375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186069375 1071 "Select the function that is continuous at $x = 2$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186069375 1071 "Select the function that is continuous at $x=2.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186069375 1074 "The correct answer is shown below.  Indeed, the graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nAll the other given graphs contain breaks at $x=2$ and it would be impossible to draw them in a neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking the pencil off of the paper." 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186069405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067570 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186069405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392702 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186069405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186069405 1071 "$\\int 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186069405 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int2x^{2}\\sin (2x)dx$?" 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186069405 1074 "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2})(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186078410 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393583 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186078445 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393506 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186078492 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392628 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186078492 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392641 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186078996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076724 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186078996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392635 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186078996 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186078996 1071 "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $[- 1, 2]$?" 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186078996 1071 "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $[-1,2]?$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186078996 1074 "The correct answer is the following. \n\n![](images/e5/e57af2e43bd292195f617f60ffed8055891531a2ca5d0249706497d7932d6639.png)\n\n      \n\nIndeed, this function is continuous on the open intervals $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is left-continuous at $x=2.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nSo, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,2]$ and  $(2,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nTherefore, this function is continuous on $[-1,2],$ because $[-1,2]$ is contained inside the interval $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $g(x)$ and $p(x)$ are not left-continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $h(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$\n\n- $q(x)$ is not defined at $x=1,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186083225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392662 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186083228 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392769 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186083230 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392776 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186083286 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393520 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186083308 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392790 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186083778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186081569 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186083778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392742 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186083778 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186083778 1071 "![](images/bb/bb30925ffaca0ce59de7508e0f3710fbb26f3a80dfbd80b689e9ee5a4cf7d699.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y = \\frac{x^{2}}{4}$ for $x ∈ [- 3, 4]$, as shown above?" 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186083778 1071 "![](images/bb/bb30925ffaca0ce59de7508e0f3710fbb26f3a80dfbd80b689e9ee5a4cf7d699.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}$ for $x\\in [-3,4],$ as shown above?" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186083778 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ $a=-3,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2x}{4}=\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{x}{2}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186087662 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393056 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186087727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393326 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186087753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393437 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186087754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393444 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186087770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393159 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186087770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393166 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186087858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392581 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186087884 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391574 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186087884 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391737 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186087885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391599 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186087885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391611 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186087895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393186 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186088153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085542 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186088153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392160 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186088153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186088153 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T$. If $T(2v_{1} + v_{2}) = \\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(v_{2}) = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -19\\end{bmatrix}$, then find $T(v_{1})$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186088153 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -19\\end{aligned}]$, then find $\\,T({v}_{1}).$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186088153 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(kv+w)=T(kv)+T(w)=kT(v)+T(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) & =2T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2}) \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =2T({v}_{1})+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -19\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 2T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -19\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 2T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186088155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085554 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186088155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392166 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186088155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186088155 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T$. If $T(v_{1} + 3v_{2}) = \\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(v_{1}) = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}$, then find $T(v_{2})$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186088155 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T({v}_{1}+3{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]$, then find $\\,T({v}_{2}).$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186088155 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v+kw)=T(v)+T(kw)=T(v)+kT(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}+3{v}_{2}) & =T({v}_{1})+3T({v}_{2}) \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]+3T({v}_{2}) \\\\ 3T({v}_{2}) & =[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 3T({v}_{2}) & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186088290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086364 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186088290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186393153 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186088290 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186088290 1071 "The velocity vector $v = ⟨u, v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n$v = ⟨5t - 8, 2t^{2} + 4⟩$.\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186088290 1071 "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨5t-8,\\,2{t}^{2}+4⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186088290 1074 "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t-8),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,\\,4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186092190 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393221 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186092226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392381 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186092239 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392016 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186092246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392588 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186092289 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392669 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186092289 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392676 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186092398 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392944 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186092398 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393368 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186092399 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392339 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186092400 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392951 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186092401 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393375 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186096637 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393256 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186096637 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393263 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186096639 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393270 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186096639 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393277 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186096681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393416 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186096752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393430 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186096816 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393207 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186096823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393284 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186096844 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392708 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186097270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095514 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186097270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392683 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186097270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186097270 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^{4}2x^{2}dx$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186097270 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{3}^{4}2{x}^{2}\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186097270 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{4}2{x}^{2}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=2{x}^{2},$ the lower bound is $a=3,$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-3}{n}=\\frac{1}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}2{x}^{2}\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(3+i\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(3+\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[2{(3+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186097308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095742 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186097308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392715 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186097308 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186097308 1071 "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2(x-1)?$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186097308 1071 "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2(x - 1)$?" 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186097308 1074 "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int (x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =2\\int (x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-1,0,1.$\n\n![](images/92/92a2dbaa5726e6a1ab83fa879ad7b630b05e68a8e366db214b74d85269a849a9.png)" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186097359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186096048 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186097359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186393410 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186097359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186097359 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y+z=1 \\\\ 2x-y-z=-1 \\\\ -2x+4y-2z=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186097359 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n${x - 2y + z = 1; 2x - y - z =-1; - 2x + 4y - 2z =-2$\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186097359 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].