68 #edb/ref 17592186391971 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186115820 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186115820 1071 "If $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$, then $A^{4} =$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186115820 1071 "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, then ${A}^{4}=$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186115820 1074 "First, to find ${A}^{2},$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${A}^{4}={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186115834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113911 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186115834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392435 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186115834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186115834 1071 "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186115834 1071 "Calculate $ABC$, where\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186115834 1074 "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, $B$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186115868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186114115 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186115868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392554 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186115868 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186115868 1071 "Find the product $E_{1}^{-1}E_{2}$, where\n$E_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, E_{2}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186115868 1071 "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186115868 1074 "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186115891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186114253 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186115891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392595 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186115891 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186115891 1071 "Which of the following functions is not differentiable over the entire interval $x ∈ (-1, 1)$ due to the existence of a vertical tangent in the interval?" 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186115891 1071 "Which of the following functions is not differentiable over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1)$ due to the existence of a vertical tangent in the interval?" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186115891 1074 "Notice that the function $y=h(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$ \nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=h(x).$\n\nThe other graphs do not have a vertical tangent inside the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0.$\n\n- The function $y=g(x)$ is smooth and continuous over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- The functions $y=p(x)$ and $y=q(x)$ are not defined at $x=0.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186119743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391623 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186119744 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393451 131941395