e\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186125091 1071 "Find ${A}^{97},$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186125091 1074 "We have been given the following diagonalization of $A.$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{97} & =P{D}^{97}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{97}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{97} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{97}\\end{aligned}](-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186125112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123242 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186125112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392147 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186125112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186125112 1071 "Given the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $f(x) = x^{22} - x^{20} + 3x - 2$, find $f(A)$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186125112 1071 "Given the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nand $\\,f(x)={x}^{22}-{x}^{20}+3x-2\\,$, find $f(A).$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186125112 1074 "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+8 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{20}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-1\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{22}\\,-\\,{x}^{20}\\,+\\,3x\\,-\\,2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{22}$ | $-$ | ${x}^{20}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $3x$ | $-$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(3x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{20}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(3A-2I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(3A-2I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(3A-2I) \\\\  & =3A-2I \\\\  & =3[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186125127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123332 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186125127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392185 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186125127 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186125127 1071 "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$ be a basis of $R^{2}$ and let $T: R^{2} → R^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[T]_{B}$ if $T(b_{1}) = 3b_{2} - 4b_{1}$ and $T(b_{2}) = 3b_{1} - b_{2}$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186125127 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}\\,$ if $\\,T({b}_{1})=3{b}_{2}-4{b}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=3{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186125127 1074 "Since we have $\\,T({b}_{1})=3{b}_{2}-4{b}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=3{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}\\,$, we have\n\n$$\n[T({b}_{1})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[T({b}_{2})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix  whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186125192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123722 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186125192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392938 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186125192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186125192 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x, y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x, y)$?\n\n1. $f_{x}(x, y) = x^{3}y - x$ and $f_{y}(x, y) = xy^{3} - y$\n2. $f_{x}(x, y) = e^{x + y} - 2y$ and $f_{y}(x, y) = e^{x + y} - 2x$\n3. $f_{x}(x, y) = \\sqrt{x - 1}$ and $f_{y}(x, y) =-3$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186125192 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={x}^{3}y-x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-y$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x+y}-2y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{x+y}-2x$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\sqrt{x-1}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-3$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186125192 1074 "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y-x)={x}^{3} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{3}-y)={y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}-2y)={e}^{x+y}-2 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y}-2x)={e}^{x+y}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x-1})=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393077 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129135 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392044 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129137 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392204 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129138 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393590 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129138 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393597 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129140 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392272 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129162 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392265 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129162 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393604 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129173 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393193 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129174 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393200 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129278 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392298 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129313 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392305 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129313 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392312 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129321 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391754 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127171 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186129620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392141 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129620 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129620 1071 "![](images/3f/3fb6039f0a1de69d41644d7153d205062e85cdfce7cdb6b6270d952f2dbab6c3.png)\n\nConsider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $y = x + 6$, $y = 2x$, and $y = 3x$, as shown in the picture. Express the double integral of the function $f(x, y)$ over the region $D$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type II regions." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186129620 1071 "![](images/3f/3fb6039f0a1de69d41644d7153d205062e85cdfce7cdb6b6270d952f2dbab6c3.png)\n\nConsider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $y=x+6,$ $y=2x,$ and $y=3x,$ as shown in the picture. Express the double integral of the function $f(x,y)$ over the region $D$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type II regions." 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129620 1074 "First, let's write our equations in terms of $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ y=x+6 & \\,⟹\\,x=y-6 \\\\ y=3x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/c4/c453d46a1f0cd6e6ddfa926de471fe38663df2bd86b9cdf4e58ce8d42462774a.png)\n\n  \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 9,\\,\\frac{y}{3}\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2}}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,9\\le y\\le 12,\\,y-6\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}{\\int }_{y/3}^{y/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{9}^{12}{\\int }_{y-6}^{y/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127267 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186129636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392179 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129636 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129636 1071 "$$\n[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129636 1071 "$[T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{bmatrix}, P_{B → C} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{B → C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186129636 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127363 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186129652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392198 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129652 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129652 1071 "If $A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 + 3\\textrm{i} & 1 + 2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 2 + \\textrm{i} & 4 + 4\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix}3 + 2\\textrm{i} & 2 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 1 + 2\\textrm{i} & 2 + 2\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$, then $AB =$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186129652 1071 "If $\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} & 1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2+\\text{i} & 4+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3+2\\text{i} & 2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1+2\\text{i} & 2+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$, then $AB=$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129652 1074 "Multiplying the matrices using the usual method, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} & 1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2+\\text{i} & 4+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3+2\\text{i} & 2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1+2\\text{i} & 2+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(2+3\\text{i})(3+2\\text{i})+(1+2\\text{i})(1+2\\text{i}) & (2+3\\text{i})(2+\\text{i})+(1+2\\text{i})(2+2\\text{i}) \\\\ (2+\\text{i})(3+2\\text{i})+(4+4\\text{i})(1+2\\text{i}) & (2+\\text{i})(2+\\text{i})+(4+4\\text{i})(2+2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13\\text{i}+(-3+4\\text{i}) & (1+8\\text{i})+(-2+6\\text{i}) \\\\ (4+7\\text{i})+(-4+12\\text{i}) & (3+4\\text{i})+16\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3+17\\text{i} & -1+14\\text{i} \\\\ 19\\text{i} & 3+20\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127621 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186129695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392218 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129695 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129695 1071 "Consider the vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2 \\cdot 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 - \\textrm{i} \\\\ 2 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 5\\textrm{i} \\\\ 2 - \\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 + 2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1 - \\textrm{i} \\\\ 3 - \\textrm{i} \\\\ -5 + 2\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $λ_{1} = 2 - \\textrm{i}$ and $λ_{3} = \\textrm{i} + 5$, respectively.\nGiven that $A = PBP^{-1}$, which of the following could be the matrix $P$?" 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186129695 1071 "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2\\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\text{i} \\\\ 2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1-\\text{i} \\\\ 3-\\text{i} \\\\ -5+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2-\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=\\text{i}+5,$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129695 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127669 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186129703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392238 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129703 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129703 1071 "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186129703 1071 "$B = {\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}, C = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}, [T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of $R^{2}$, and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186129703 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 11 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -5 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 11 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 11 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 7-2\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -18 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186133756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392388 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186133762 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392567 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186133767 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393403 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186133776 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393396 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186133789 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392319 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186133794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392360 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186133796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392374 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186133805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392428 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186133806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392408 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186133830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392326 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186133835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393624 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186133835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393631 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186133848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392346 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186133867 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392421 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186133868 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392395 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131989 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186134330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392402 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134330 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134330 1071 "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134330 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(A) = \\begin{bmatrix}q_{11} & q_{12} \\\\ q_{21} & q 22 \\\\ q_{31} & q_{32}\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(Q)\\begin{bmatrix}r_{11} & r 12 \\\\ 0 & r_{22}\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(R)$.\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns $q_{1}, q_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find the value of $q_{22} \\cdot r_{12}$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186134330 1074 "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{12}{6})[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{6})=2\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132175 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186134361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392415 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134361 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ will give a QR factorization of $A.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134361 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, Q = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A = QR$ will give a QR factorization of $A$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186134361 1074 "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134384 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132313 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186134384 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392501 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134384 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134384 1071 "![](images/6f/6f51a25843259881994e3dca0781b780878bec349930b76fbf449dc824e69ec2.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t) = ⟨2\\sin t, 3\\cos t⟩$ for $t ∈ [0, 2π]$.\nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$Area(D) = ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}AAAAAAAdt$,\n\nwhere $P(x, y) =-y$ and $Q(x, y) = 0$. Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186134384 1071 "![](images/6f/6f51a25843259881994e3dca0781b780878bec349930b76fbf449dc824e69ec2.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134384 1074 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134413 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132487 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186134413 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392528 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134413 1071 "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0$?\n\n$\\begin{vmatrix}A|= & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3}; \\sqrt[5]{2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}, & B|= & 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}4; 0\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}, & C|= & 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}4; 2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186134413 1071 "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt[5]{2} & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|B|=|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|,\\,|C|=|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134413 1074 "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has a zero row: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt[5]{2} & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|A|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a triangular matrix, hence \n\n$$\n|B|=1\\cdot 3\\cdot 4=12\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has proportional columns: \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|C|=0.$\n\nTherefore, only $|A|$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133038 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186134505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186393091 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134505 1071 "Given that $u = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}$, find the value of $\\frac{c}{a + b}$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186134505 1071 "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{c}{a+b}.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186134505 1074 "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=1,$ and $c=2.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{c}{a+b}=\\frac{2}{1+1}=1.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186138485 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392783 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186147432 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393125 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186147432 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393132 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186147443 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392911 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186147443 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392918 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186147444 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392843 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186147445 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392856 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186147446 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392863 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186147453 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392876 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186147503 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391761 131