otal surface area of the square pyramid above using its net." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186147980 1074 "The net of our pyramid is the following.\n\n![](images/09/09eff98ca31b4bb350e2d793dcfa6e6972de25bb3b613c898533068c36fe9a78.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the base of the pyramid is a square with a side equal to $2\\,\\text{m}.$ Hence:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the square}=2\\times 2=4\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEach side face of our solid is triangle, where the base is $2\\,\\text{m}$ and the height is $9\\,\\text{m}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the triangle}=\\frac{1}{2}\\times 2\\times 9=9\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the surface area of the pyramid consists of $1$ square (base) and $4$ triangles (side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =(\\text{Area of the square})+4\\times (\\text{Area of the triangle}) \\\\  & =4+4\\times 9 \\\\  & =4+36 \\\\  & =40\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186148161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146690 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186148161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392837 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186148161 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186148161 1071 "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x = 1$, in ascending powers of $(x - 1)$ up to and including the term in $(x - 1)^{3}$, of the initial value problem\n$y^{″} = xy + y^{′}, y(1) = 1, y^{′}(1) = 2$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186148161 1071 "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=1$, in ascending powers of $(x-1)$ up to and including the term in $(x-1)^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=xy+{y}^{′},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2.\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186148161 1074 "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=1$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(1)+(x-1){y}^{′}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(1)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(1)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(1).$\n\nWe substitute $y(1)=1$ and ${y}^{′}(1)=2$ into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(1) & =1\\cdot y(1)+{y}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)=x{y}^{′}+y+{y}^{″}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(1)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(1) & =1\\cdot {y}^{′}(1)+y(1)+{y}^{″}(1) \\\\  & =2+1+3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(1)+(x-1){y}^{′}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(1)+⋯ \\\\  & =1+(x-1)\\cdot 2+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}\\cdot 3+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{6}\\cdot 6+… \\\\  & =1+2(x-1)+\\frac{3}{2}(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186148178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146792 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186148178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392850 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186148178 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186148178 1071 "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x = π$, in ascending powers of $(x - π)$ up to and including the term in $(x - π)^{3}$, of the initial value problem\n$y^{″} = y\\cos x, y(π) = 1, y^{′}(π) = 2$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186148178 1071 "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=\\pi $, in ascending powers of $(x-\\pi )$ up to and including the term in $(x-\\pi )^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(\\pi )=1,\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )=2.\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186148178 1074 "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=\\pi $:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(\\pi )+(x-\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi )+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(\\pi )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\pi ),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(\\pi )$ and ${y}^{(3)}(\\pi ).$\n\nWe substitute $y(\\pi )=1$ and ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=2$ into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(\\pi ) & =y(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)={y}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(\\pi )$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi ) & ={y}^{′}(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-y(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1))-(1\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(\\pi )+(x-\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi )+⋯ \\\\  & =1+(x-\\pi )\\cdot 2+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2}\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{6}\\cdot (-2)+… \\\\  & =1+2(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186148180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146804 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186148180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392870 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186148180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186148180 1071 "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 4y = 8t^{2}$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $1$ second." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186148180 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=8{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $1$ second." 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186148180 1074 "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {t}^{2}+\\beta t+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2,\\beta =0,\\gamma =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2{t}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=1,\\,B=0.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+2{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after one second, we substitute into the equation $t=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2(1)+2(1)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2+1)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393527 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152165 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393638 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152165 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393645 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152173 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393576 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152173 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393729 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152173 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393736 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152181 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392925 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152238 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393486 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391414 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391426 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149855 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186152573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149858 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186152573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149861 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186152573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391207 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391210 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391213 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152573 1071 "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x+1},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152573 1071 "$y_{1}(x) = e^{2x + 1}, y_{2}(x) = e^{2x}, x ∈ R$\nConsider the functions $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ above. Given that $y_{1}$ and $y_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below. The Wronskian is $W(y_{1}, y_{2}) =$\n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, the set ${y_{1}(x), y_{2}(x)}$ is linearly\n{{field-3}}\non $R$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186152573 1074 "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ {({e}^{2x+1})}^{′} & {({e}^{2x})}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x+1} & 2{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{2x+1}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x+1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{4x+1}-2{e}^{4x+1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150293 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186152636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392932 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152636 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152636 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+3y=2{t}^{4}{e}^{t},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nhas the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(t)=At+B{t}^{3}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ to this equation.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\int {t}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=({t}^{2}-2t+2){e}^{t}+C\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152636 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$t^{2}y^{″} - 3ty^{′} + 3y = 2t^{4}e^{t}, t > 0$\n\nhas the complimentary function $y_{c}(t) = At + Bt^{3}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(t)$ to this equation.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n$\\intt^{2}e^{t}dt = (t^{2} - 2t + 2)e^{t} + C$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186152636 1074 "First, we reduce the differential equation to the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(t){y}^{′}+Q(t)y=f(t).\n$$\n\nSo, we divide both sides of the given equation by ${t}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-\\frac{3}{t}{y}^{′}+\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}y=2{t}^{2}{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe notice that the forcing term $f(t)=2{t}^{2}{e}^{t}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions:\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(t)({t}^{3})^{′}-{(t)}^{′}({t}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{t}^{3}-{t}^{3} \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are independent.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(t) & ={y}_{1}(t)u(t)+{y}_{2}(t)v(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =-\\int \\frac{f(t){y}_{2}(t)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}t=-\\int {t}^{2}{e}^{t}=-({t}^{2}-2t+2){e}^{t}, \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{f(t){y}_{1}(t)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(t) & ={y}_{1}(t)u(t)+{y}_{2}(t)v(t) \\\\  & =-t({t}^{2}-2t+2){e}^{t}+{t}^{3}{e}^{t} \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}-2t){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150971 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186152749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186393618 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152749 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152749 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)x^{2}yds$, where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r = \\sqrt{2}$ for $-\\frac{π}{4} \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{4}$?" 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186152749 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186152749 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{2})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186157283 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392353 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186157455 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391379 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186157455 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391386 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186157455 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391400 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186157457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391452 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186157457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391459 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186157463 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391671 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186166904 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392291 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186166906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393652 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186166906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393659 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186166906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393666 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186166920 1038 #edb/ref 175