ns above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$\n$y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-2}} is {{field-3}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}." 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186167245 1071 "${x^{′}(t) =-3x(t) - 2y(t); y^{′}(t) =-x(t) - 2y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1} =-4$ and $λ_{2} =-1$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $v_{1} = [2, 1]^{T}$ and $v_{2} = [- 1, 1]^{T}$, respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n$y = \\frac{1}{2}x$ is {{field-1}} line.\n\n{{field-2}} is {{field-3}} line.\n\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186167245 1074 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/63/6337a670b6fa73c4c21759b600269cb83d808a1c13026c90e2d02519fa31cd67.png)" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186167301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164991 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186167301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391820 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186167301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391825 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186167301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391830 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186167301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391834 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186167301 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186167301 1071 "Consider the function $F(x) = \\mid x \\mid$ defined on $x ∈ [- π, π)$. The Fourier series of the $2π$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n$f(x)∼\\frac{π}{2} - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(4)/(π(2n - 1)^{2})\\cos ((2n - 1)x)$.\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.At $x = 0$, we have\n$(-1)^{n + 1}$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186167301 1071 "Consider the function $F(x)=|x|$ defined on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{\\pi }{2}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{\\pi (2n-1)^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.At $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot 0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}}={{field-4}}.\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186167301 1074 "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot 0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot 0) \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\ -\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}} & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186167321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165105 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186167321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392279 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186167321 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186167321 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186167321 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t), x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186167321 1074 "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186167322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165111 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186167322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392285 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186167322 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186167322 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186167322 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\ln (2)\\end{bmatrix}x(t), x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186167322 1074 "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{2}^{0} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =5[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186179674 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393534 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186180279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186179188 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186180279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186393493 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186180279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186180279 1071 "Let $R = [1, 3] \\cdot [1, 3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = 10 - 2x - y$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nGiven that the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n$U(f, P) = ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)AAAAAΔx_{i}Δy_{j}$\nwhere $Δx_{i} = x_{i} - x_{i - 1}$ and $Δy_{j} = y_{j} - y_{j - 1}$, what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186180279 1071 "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=10-2x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186180279 1074 "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=10-2x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=10-2x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/ce/ce0d90127e1f14654f4b32a933f1eb6e76e054c03f19ca40c3f04116aa3c2c93.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/5b/5b9967c814e46914e0409854de0960e85339adc9b9fd9693677c8e048c342702.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j-1})=10-2{x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(10-2{x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $10-2{x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j-1}.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186184166 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391964 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186184182 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392367 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186184781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183265 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186184781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392333 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186184781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186184781 1071 "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}],\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186184781 1071 "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}, {x_{1} - 3x_{2} + 2x_{3} = 0; 3x_{1} - 9x_{2} + 6x_{3} = 0$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186184781 1074 "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -9 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}10 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186198361 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392728 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186198362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392735 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186198479 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391896 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186217249 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391354 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186217249 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391438 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186217249 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391445 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186217256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391660 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186217788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216567 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186217788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391286 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186217788 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186217788 1071 "Given that $y_{0}(x) = \\frac{2}{x}$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$y^{′} = y^{2} - \\frac{4}{x}y + \\frac{2}{x^{2}}$\n\nfind the general solution of the equation." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186217788 1071 "Given that ${y}_{0}(x)=\\frac{2}{x}$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x}y+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nfind the general solution of the equation." 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186217788 1074 "The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x}y+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x)=1,\\,b(x)=-\\frac{4}{x},\\,c(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+\\frac{2}{x}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}=(z+\\frac{2}{x})^{2}-\\frac{4}{x}(z+\\frac{2}{x})+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & =({z}^{2}+\\frac{4z}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})-(\\frac{4z}{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & ={z}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & =x+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z=y-\\frac{2}{x}$ to recover the initial variable $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{x+C} \\\\ y-\\frac{2}{x} & =-\\frac{1}{x+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{x+C}+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186217798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216603 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186217798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216608 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186217798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391644 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186217798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391649 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186217798 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186217798 1071 "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n(\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186217798 1071 "Consider the inexact differential equation\n$(\\frac{6y^{2}}{x} + 4y^{3})dx + (4y + 3xy^{2} + \\frac{1}{x^{3}})dy = 0$.\nIt is known that\n$\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) = \\frac{3}{x}$\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n\n$P(x, y) = \\frac{6y^{2}}{x} + 4y^{3}, Q(x, y) = 4y + 3xy^{2} + \\frac{1}{x^{3}}$.\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\nA suitable integrating factor is $I(x) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." 13194139533464 17592186045585 false] [17592186217798 1074 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int 3{x}^{-1}\\text{ }\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}[(\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{(6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{(4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y=c.\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186221407 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391493 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186221412 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391292 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186221412 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391299 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186221412 1038 #edb/re