field-3}}.\nThus, we must have \n{{field-4}}." 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391466 1072 #uuid "ade9d10e-8ab5-5b95-969f-539a44434240" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391466 1074 "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that the given curve is $\\text{bounded}$ (and closed), representing $\\text{an ellipse}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nBut we have that:\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a>0,$ and\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a<0.$\n\nThus, we must have $a=0.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391467 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391468 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391467 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391469 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391467 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391467 1077 "field-1" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391467 1079 #edb/ref 17592186391469 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391467 1080 #uuid "aafde8dd-f176-57c0-adc4-2c7ebf5d023e" 13194139533464 175921