186391485 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391485 1084 #uuid "f41f25dd-037b-571c-8c29-af5ee91636ff" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391486 1069 "q-334532" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391486 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391486 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[-1,\\text{ }2]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391486 1072 #uuid "8cb0146a-edb8-5224-b29c-36d6ad8aec81" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391486 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-2x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c3afc4bedb411c778773190a3b9bc1db0b376dd69592ba744e8225ec900cc45.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-t-1 \\\\ y=2t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/0d/0d506993d3bfbb727dffd44ced1a588b40e2fefa86e7f2780d90277c9d0a9eed.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t+1 \\\\ y=-2t-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/e8/e88dfa02c57a5c31df14fdf6f963f7d568eaebccbbbfba6d8f2958593ac4693a.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/3f/3f80cbae958772e0fa0492f5c426d412f002c3baa41a8a69b38e594512823422.png)" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391487 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391488 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391487 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391489 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391487 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391490 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391487 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391491 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391487 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391492 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391487 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391487 1077 "selection" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391487 1079 #edb/ref 17592186391490 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391487 1080 #uuid "0b27342d-f535-5330-acd6-ae18222b5178" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391488 1081 "images/1d/1dca375ebe6f8468f12a1729d697d8d35ff6b430a70e53639ab029254ac392fa.png" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391488 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391488 1084 #uuid "a2916d28-5efb-5314-8923-0573bec1151e" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391489 1081 "images/87/879cc00fedd506a0607bce3d3d36f99b489f599dadb6b13d4d1176fa24441853.png" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391489 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391489 1084 #uuid "ea635c52-25f9-5aa9-95df-3db5bd2c8c5c" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391490 1081 "images/3f/3f80cbae958772e0fa0492f5c426d412f002c3baa41a8a69b38e594512823422.png" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391490 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391490 1084 #uuid "1757ec4c-4154-59ad-82df-49a519e58859" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391491 1081 "images/c9/c9a56a6a1f17d58226fb2fac905247b339be111335637a15d3e8c447aab3f3a8.png" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391491 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391491 1084 #uuid "20b254ca-ca7c-577b-af73-4554ed77ae3b" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391492 1081 "images/8a/8a178460d4e87756694e03bc37dd58c3e70cf3222389b31fe838aeb6a910b984.png" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391492 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391492 1084 #uuid "de04ae64-037b-57a5-a073-94201161c12a" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391494 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391500 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391506 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391512 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391493 1069 "q-334567" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391493 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391493 1071 "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391493 1072 #uuid "db1b2cc2-9ed0-52dd-b570-f28962fe5ef0" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391493 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.6) & =2(0.6)^{4}-(0.6)^{5} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.129\\,6-1\\cdot 0.077\\,76 \\\\  & =0.259\\,2-0.077\\,76 \\\\  & =0.181\\,44 \\\\  & ≈0.181.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =2(0.2)^{4}-(0.2)^{5} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.001\\,6-1\\cdot 0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.003\\,2-0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.002\\,88 \\\\  & ≈0.003.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.2}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={3}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.181\\,44$ to $0.002\\,88.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.181\\,44}{0.002\\,88}=63≈63.000.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈63 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(63) \\\\ p & ≈3.771….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391495 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391496 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391497 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391498 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391494 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391494 1077 "field-2" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391494 1079 #edb/ref 17592186391495 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391494 1080 #uuid "651a4ec8-2dbb-5846-aa30-211a9e7851fe" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391495 1081 "0.003" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391495 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391495 1084 #uuid "731b0949-2836-51f7-a241-1c29cd44179b" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391496 1081 "0.257" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391496 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391496 1084 #uuid "f4b137cf-d2c6-5334-b289-2da9c0b93f17" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391497 1081 "0.042" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391497 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391497 1084 #uuid "aed5