91516 1081 "4" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391516 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391516 1084 #uuid "de729150-01d6-5f51-9aff-702963293c6c" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391517 1081 "5" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391517 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391517 1084 #uuid "83c114ba-a0a8-593b-84b8-95a0ae1fe8d1" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391519 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391518 1069 "q-334639" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391518 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+\\frac{1}{2}y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[-1,\\text{ }2]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[-1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391518 1072 #uuid "c07d6a46-4ba6-5448-9f13-79808c35273d" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391518 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-2x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/60/6044d391016e37af929b36e89e0a802151b5710707cc510e9e7f70bf9a2d1c0d.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-t-2 \\\\ y=2t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/54/54bdfaf14b4f580e1476d4649e03cd71cb8203e23ae318daa65251fd7ce02ba1.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t+2 \\\\ y=-2t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/ed/edb5558ed7b5ee0e4aacb4eb67d74747fcc366f1508e16b3e99f3e0d5ee53306.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/10/1005575ad1d73527ce1bd4e946aa9a3d0be86ae82329fde27700082ea2b5c121.png)" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391519 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391520 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391519 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391521 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391519 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391522 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391519 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391523 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391519 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391524 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391519 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [1759218639151