rue] [17592186391525 1069 "q-334981" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391525 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391525 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-3x+4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=2y-xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Select the correct options in the following statement.$\\,$\nTerm ${y}^{′}$ in the second equation represents the \n{{field-1}} \nof the \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391525 1072 #uuid "e000d57d-98f9-55aa-b3de-818fe5192c62" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391525 1074 "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a $\\text{rate of change}.$\n\nFor the ${y}^{′}$ equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y-xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$ must represent the $\\text{prey}.$ Indeed, \n\n- it grows exponentially in the absence of predators (the $2y$ term), and\n\n- decreases when interacting with predators (the $-xy$ term)." 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391526 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391527 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391526 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391528 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391526 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391526 1077 "field-1" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391526 1079 #edb/ref 17592186391527 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391526 1080 #uuid "a3d29eb0-e081-5535-a8d8-d24758a7c44b" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391527 1081 "rate of change" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391527 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391527 1084 #uuid "cdb4ed4e-5982-595b-8411-719af1fc8daa" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391528 1081 "amount" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391528 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391528 1084 #uuid "930e955f-f486-5b79-ac37-e2bb7d995b1c" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391529 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391530 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391529 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391531 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391529 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391529 1077 "field-2" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391529 1079 #edb/ref 17592186391531 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391529 1080 #uuid "49e11686-e80d-596d-a5fd-7116689372d8" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391530 1081 "predator" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391530 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391530 1084 #uuid "eefd88b6-5ed8-5e56-97a5-84f5e5b9ced2" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391531 1081 "prey" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391531 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391531 1084 #uuid "0160babf-a013-59e1-b565-159549cd8ce3" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391533 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391539 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391544 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391532 1069 "q-335179" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391532 1071 "A research container of mass $20\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the container's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the descent began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the container is proportional to its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k>0$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the container’s velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration is $8.2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nFind the constant of proportionality $k,$ and the differential equation that best models the velocity. Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the container are weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n$\\,$ The constant of proportionality is $k=$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ The differential equation that best models the velocity is\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391532 1072 #uuid "64f5cd17-fe8c-5f61-947e-78b369ea5b30" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391532 1074 "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, there are two forces acting on the body:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the body, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =20g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity of the body (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Note that, since the drag, in $N=\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ is the product of the constant $k$ and velocity, in $\\text{m/s},$ the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{N}}{\\text{m/s}}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2}}{\\text{m/s}}=\\text{kg/s}.\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=20g-kv.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the body, we have\n\n$$\n20g-kv=20\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}.\n$$\n\nWriting in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}+kv & =20g \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{k}{20}v & =g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we're given that, at a particular moment, the body's velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration is $8.2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\nSo, substituting $v=8,g=9.8,$ and $\\frac{dv}{dt}=8.2$ into our model, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8.2+\\frac{k}{20}\\cdot 8 & =9.8 \\\\ \\frac{2k}{5} & =1.6 \\\\ k & =4\\,\\text{kg/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation that models the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{5}v=g.\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391533 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391534 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391533 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391535 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391533 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391536 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391533 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391537 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391533 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391538 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391533 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391533 1077 "field-2" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391533 1079 #edb/ref 17592186391534 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391533 1080 #uuid "c4490809-5163-5471-a1d4-a55afc28a312" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391534 1081 "\\text{kg/s}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391534 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391534 1084 #uuid "ee9ad499-5aa4-5a3a-acbc-e49ac21406a7" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391535 1081 "\\text{s/m}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391535 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391535 1084 #uuid "b2955172-b81c-5011-9701-e9bf938ea274" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391536 1081 "{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391536 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391536 1084 #uuid "decbd6c