53da92ca-2c6d-5b4b-91fd-641e504d29e4" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391556 1074 "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, two forces are acting on the container:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the container, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =10g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the square of the velocity of the container (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Note that, since drag, in $\\text{N}=\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ is the product of the constant $k$ and squared velocity, in $(\\text{m/s})^{2}={\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{N}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2}}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2}}=\\text{kg/m}.\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the container is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=10g-k{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the container, we get\n\n$$\n10g-k{v}^{2}=10\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{k}{10}{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we're given that, at a particular moment, the container's velocity is $5\\,\\text{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration is $4.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ So, substituting $v=5,g=9.8,$ and $\\frac{dv}{dt}=4.8$ into our model, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4.8 & =9.8-\\frac{k}{10}\\cdot {5}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}k & =5 \\\\ k & =2\\,\\text{kg/m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation that models the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{1}{5}{v}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [1