[17592186391574 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=6{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391574 1072 #uuid "6530e5db-b521-5f5c-99f7-59872d852e59" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391574 1074 "This equation is already in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=-3$ and $Q(x)=6{e}^{2x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{-3x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y) & ={e}^{-3x}\\cdot 6{e}^{2x} \\\\ {e}^{-3x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{e}^{-3x}\\cdot y & =6{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-3x}) & =6{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-3x})\\text{d}x & =\\int 6{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{-3x} & =-6{e}^{-x}+C \\\\ y & =-6{e}^{2x}+C{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391575 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391576 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391575 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391577 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391575 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391578 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391575 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391579 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391575 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391575 1077 "field-2" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391575 1079 #edb/ref 175921