45585 true] [17592186391660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391661 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391666 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391660 1069 "q-338027" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391660 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391660 1071 "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n(3x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+\\frac{3}{x})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=3x+{y}^{2},\\,Q(x,y)=xy+\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391660 1072 #uuid "f5b12f52-bca9-597a-88b5-c5036d3e9da3" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391660 1074 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int {x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x[(3x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+\\frac{3}{x})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{(3{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{({x}^{2}y+3)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)={x}^{2}y+3.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (3{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}y+3)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+3y+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=3y,\\,G(x)={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+3y=c.\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391661 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391662 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391661 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391663 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391661 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391664 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391661 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391665 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391661 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391661 1077 "field-1" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391661 1079 #edb/ref 17592186391664 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391661 1080 #uuid "20192922-8171-54fa-90a2-ddc855fb0da5" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391662 1081 "\\frac{1}{x}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391662 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [1