4 1084 #uuid "56ed424b-8ca9-59d2-8528-1ebfe5b468f4" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391715 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391716 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391715 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391721 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391715 1069 "q-338916" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391715 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391715 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16\\lambda y=0,\\,y(0)=y(5)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 5,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391715 1072 #uuid "72f2d583-8ffc-50a1-bdb5-e2d5a11cd167" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391715 1074 "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=y(5)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(5)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(20\\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n20\\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{400}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391716 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391717 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391716 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391718 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391716 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391719 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391716 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391720 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391716 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391716 1077 "field-1" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391716 1079 #edb/ref 17592186391718 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391716 1080 #uuid "77dfda2b-2332-5cb1-bf2d-c957e336718a" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391717 1081 "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{20}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391717 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391717 1084 #uuid "66f6bef1-778e-54ac-8bca-389bd469b61a" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391718 1081 "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{400}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391718 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391718 1084 #uuid "2b8245c8-0e8a-5ec6-812c-40fb520a0dfb" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391719 1081 "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{100}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 t