frac{s+1}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ First, add $2$ to the right-hand side and find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s+1)Y(s) & =2+\\frac{s+1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{2({s}^{2}+1)}{{s}^{2}+1}+\\frac{s+1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}+s+3}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing by $(s+1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{2{s}^{2}+s+3}{(s+1)({s}^{2}+1)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. Since the denominator has a linear factor $(s+1)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{s}^{2}+s+3}{(s+1)({s}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{A}{s+1}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n2{s}^{2}+s+3=A({s}^{2}+1)+(Bs+C)(s+1)\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-1,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n2(-1)^{2}+(-1)+3=A((-1)^{2}+1)+0\\,⟹\\,4=2A\\,⟹\\,A=2.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n  \n\n$$\n2=A+B\\,⟹\\,2=2+B\\,⟹\\,B=0.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n \n\n$$\n3=A+C\\,⟹\\,3=2+C\\,⟹\\,C=1.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{2}{s+1}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+1}} & ={e}^{-t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+1}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} \\\\ y(t) & =2{e}^{-t}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391855 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391856 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391855 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391857 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391855 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391858 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391855 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391859 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391855 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533464 1759218604558