a5633a39368a" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391877 1074 "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, and we have one free variables, ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$\n\nFrom the second equation we get ${x}_{2}=4{x}_{3}.$ Substituting ${x}_{2}=4{x}_{3}$ into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(4{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-11{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the set of all the solutions of $Ax=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}-11{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{3}\\in R}.\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391878 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391879 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391878 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391880 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391878 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391881 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391878 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391882 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391878 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391883 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391878 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391878 1077 "selection" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391878 107