068 #edb/ref 17592186391991 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391990 1069 "q-36398" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391990 1071 "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]}$ and $C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391990 1072 #uuid "f7e547d0-2041-562b-9514-c21517672564" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391990 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -8 & 3 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391991 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391992 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186391991 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391993 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [1