194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392016 1071 "Find an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}16 & -30 \\\\ 9 & -17\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392016 1072 #uuid "60baa1cc-5bec-5c5e-b7f7-4efe1f7a3de3" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392016 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -30 \\\\ 9 & -17\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -30 \\\\ 9 & -15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}18{x}_{1}-30{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}-15{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -30 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -15 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}18 & -30 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n18{x}_{1}-30{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392017 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392018 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392017 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392019 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392017 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392020 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [175921