 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392339 1069 "q-40004" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392339 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392339 1071 "The first term of a geometric sequence is $\\frac{5}{3}$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{4}{3}.$ Written in sigma notation, what is the sum of the first $25$ terms?" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392339 1072 #uuid "23fc1c87-6114-5e7d-b310-49216689236b" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392339 1074 "First, we must express the series in the form:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3},$ $r=\\frac{4}{3}$ and $N=25.$\n\nExpressing the geometric series in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{S}_{25}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{25}\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify the above using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{25}\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{25}\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n}\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{25}\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{-1}\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{25}\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{25}\\frac{5}{4}{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392340 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392341 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392340 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392342 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392340 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392343 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392340 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392344 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392340 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392345 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392340 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533464