sider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392346 1072 #uuid "b8a18aba-5bf5-5598-9a5a-e92c825d1e00" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392346 1074 "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=\\frac{11}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{11}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(4[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+11[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392347 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392348 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392347 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392349 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392347 1075 #edb/ref 