ollowing functions is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]?$  You may assume that all points of discontinuity for each function are shown." 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392641 1072 #uuid "c74056bd-b0fc-5dc2-914f-2bbc6b987726" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392641 1074 "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](images/72/7225a16c8dfa80bc750607b30c6deb30bb82807365c0c975c6b5ae3e33e11c6f.png)\n\nWe see that $h(x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,0],$ and since $(-\\infty ,-1]$ lies completely inside $(-\\infty ,0],$ it is also continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are not continuous at $x=-2$, and so are not continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1].$\n\n- $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are not continuous at $x=-3$, and so are not continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1].$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392643 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392644 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392645 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392646 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392647 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392642 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392642 1077 "selection" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392642 1079 #edb/ref 17592186392643 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392642 1080 #uuid "d4d2a845-2450-5e73-b0b2-bcfee6b03964" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392643 1081 "images/72/7225a16c8dfa80bc750607b30c6deb30bb82807365c0c975c6b5ae3e33e11c6f.png" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392643 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392643 1084 #uuid "10ef9b73-f52e-5b9c-9db2-9010a5191176" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392644 1081 "images/da/da6eb0e1cc28a0bef252e0d66bb232006f6a6e54142b27bb04401ca97025af30.png" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392644 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392644 1084 #uuid "d19ed205-b0ad-5bbb-bcec-dfa49650fec0" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392645 1081 "images/6c/6c04be0224db916731c3f7b4e2093887ffd36a258b19c4112766125ff435704c.png" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392645 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392645 1084 #uuid "f034c628-7593-57d9-af24-2336a12e680e" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392646 1081 "images/49/49356dbd9984d40899b321698d641f200d1d28d498323aa88fdfdfac53ed7910.png" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392646 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392646 1084 #uuid "899a69d0-95f3-5220-8187-28e906306a43" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392647 1081 "images/13/1326b043af92c5945a5fa679f7daa57eb269ae13edb6d3f9296b3a522678039c.png" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392647 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392647 1084 #uuid "c336072e-08e4-5982-8f58-51b86e8995ab" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392649 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392648 1069 "q-48381" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392648 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392648 1071 "The sides (in feet) of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to four times the rate of increase of the diagonals. What is the length of the diagonals of the square at that moment?" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392648 1072 #uuid "7963534b-05e1-5074-b217-dce61952496a" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392648 1074 "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=4\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$, so replacing $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 4\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 4 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have that the length of the diagonals of the square is $4\\text{ feet}.$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392649 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392650 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392649 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392651 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392649 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392652 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392649 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392653 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392649 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392654 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392649 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392649 1077 "selection" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392649 1079 #edb/ref 17592186392653 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392649 1080 #uuid "50391d24-edeb-556b-9cad-499093215c2a" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392650 1081 "12.5\\text{ feet}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392650 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392650 1084 #uuid "797a4f0c-845d-5bd4-a00e-f4042c09b16b" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392651 1081 "6\\text{ feet}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392651 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392651 1084 #uuid "c6bd86db-1af1-529e-b7e3-3d8c2f9d1330" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392652 1081 "8\\text{ feet}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392652 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392652 1084 #uuid "4ff2b416-758c-5d4f-944b-1eac169b1cce" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392653 1081 "4\\text{ feet}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392653 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392653 1084 #uuid "bb340c74-6d19-51d7-b6b4-c00f66ac9476" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392654 1081 "5.5\\text{ feet}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392654 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392654 1084 #uuid "71d4db78-dc17-5a6a-ab36-7c6870d8b79b" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392656 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392655 1069 "q-48399" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392655 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392655 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2]?$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392655 1072 #uuid "2b51b4f8-9d77-5aa5-bb70-80904cd708eb" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392655 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $t(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=1$ and is not defined for $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)$  is continuous on $[0,2]$ but has a cusp at $x=1.$  Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,2).$  Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$ Hence, it  is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $s(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ but has a corner point at $x=1.$   Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,2).$  Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem." 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392656 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392657 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392656 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392658 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392656 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392659 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392656 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392660 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392656 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392661 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392656 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392656 1077 "selection" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392656 1079 #edb/ref 17592186392661 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392656 1080 #uuid "37587bec-ab50-5693-886d-2f7966abfa74" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392657 1081 "images/36/36a329b531e3d48d82585ee336b10dff8b4b78585829a56d72cdac61e0aba45d.png" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392657 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392657 1084 #uuid "4416f6af-e7e0-5f20-baa8-a14cec8e89ff" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392658 1081 "images/66/66663e046f3c00d3f4884b7129cdc5b5d4be18574c6f2fc47fd39