method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3y+4{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392843 1072 #uuid "3d1d32a0-6249-5ad9-baca-e7fe99dac145" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392843 1074 "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=-2,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =3y(0)+4{e}^{2(0)} \\\\  & =3(-2)+4(1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}=3{y}^{′}+8{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & =3{y}^{′}(0)+8{e}^{2(0)} \\\\  & =3(0)+8 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinaly, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =-2+x\\cdot 0+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}\\cdot (-2)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}\\cdot 8+⋯ \\\\  & =-2-{x}^{2}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392844 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392845 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392844 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392846 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392844 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392847 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392844 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392848 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392844 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392849 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392844 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533464 17592186045