ef 17592186392857 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392856 1069 "q-64564" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392856 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392856 1071 "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=1$, in ascending powers of $(x-1)$ up to and including the term in $(x-1)^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=y{e}^{1-x},\\,y(1)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0.\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392856 1072 #uuid "9b925fd6-63c4-512c-989c-41491a7f83b1" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392856 1074 "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=1$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(1)+(x-1){y}^{′}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(1)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(1)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(1).$\n\nWe substitute $y(1)=-1$ and ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(1) & =y(1){e}^{1-1} \\\\  & =(-1){e}^{0} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)={y}^{′}{e}^{1-x}-y{e}^{1-x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(1)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(1) & ={y}^{′}(1){e}^{1-1}-y(1){e}^{1-1} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot {e}^{0})-(-1\\cdot {e}^{0}) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(1)+(x-1){y}^{′}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(1)+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+(x-1)\\cdot 0+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{6}\\cdot 1+… \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}(x-1)^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392857 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392858 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392857 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392859 13