73 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392873 1084 #uuid "bc71a800-3396-5d41-b999-dc1cc70c1cd8" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392874 1081 "y(1)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2+3)\\,\\text{m}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392874 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392874 1084 #uuid "d183e142-084c-5c40-a40a-3e43c9bd291f" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392875 1081 "y(1)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3-1)\\,\\text{m}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392875 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392875 1084 #uuid "0d2b2af7-c2a5-5791-bb97-7ee22ccd83a1" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392876 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392877 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392876 1069 "q-64623" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392876 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392876 1071 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+52y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392876 1072 #uuid "9e801805-48d7-5f7f-a2eb-42e2eeee70b1" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392876 1074 "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +52=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +52 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16)-16+52 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2}+36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =-36 \\\\ \\lambda +4 & =\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4\\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=6\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392877 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392878 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392877 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392879 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392877 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392880 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392877 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392881 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392877 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392882 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392877 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392877 1077 "selection" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392877 1079 #edb/ref 17592186392878 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392877 1080 #uuid "f29649ee-a16d-56d9-80ba-8cd64f7d549a" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392878 1081 "\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392878 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392878 1084 #uuid "9d7c71da-e88e-5ef7-9992-57e3285741a8" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392879 1081 "3\\pi \\,\\text{s}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392879 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392879 1084 #uuid "87ab7734-6709-5366-9804-65686fe38f5e" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392880 1081 "6\\pi \\,\\text{s}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392880 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392880 1084 #uuid "bc219892-8db2-59ec-8f0e-74228c0dcdd5" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392881 1081 "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392881 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392881 1084 #uuid "90aaa559-d83e-58cb-8b19-dca2ca99dcd5" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392882 1081 "\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392882 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392882 1084 #uuid "af845d3e-6ea7-51b2-9edf-f53410cbddd8" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392884 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392883 1069 "q-64717" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392883 1071 "![](images/36/36bbd69dec59657a74992dbddced8b87190f953687e0d84aa8f3081e8754789c.png)\n\nWhich of the following could represent the level curves of the surface $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ shown above?" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392883 1072 #uuid "c2b2fff3-d401-5946-82c7-5c2974317dd2" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392883 1074 "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[-1,1].$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\in [-1,1].$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =c \\\\ y & =\\pm \\operatorname{arccos}⁡c+2\\pi n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ can be any integer. \n\nNotice that in the equations $y=\\pm \\operatorname{arccos}⁡c+2\\pi n$ do not depend on $x.$ So, for a given $c\\in [-1,1]$ and integer $n$, the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of horizontal lines, as shown below.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/a8/a8c4e910e3884ee68518752ab814f491edb14d422b4c25926b7d3ad34720592d.png)" 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392884 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392885 13194139533464 17592186045585 true] [17592186392884 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392886 131941